B.a9B=new A.i(15,"caution","ATROPINE IS THE PESTICIDE ANTIDOTE AND IT IS GIVEN IN HOSPITAL - the article notes a hospital may get through very large amounts of it, running into hundreds of milligrams. It also describes a diagnostic trial: if the child improves after 0.6 mg to 1 mg of atropine, that points towards poisoning by a cholinesterase inhibitor. Those are resuscitation-room numbers.")
B.a8Q=new A.i(15,"caution","THE PARACETAMOL ANTIDOTE IS TIME-CRITICAL - acetylcysteine, usually intravenous, is the antidote that matters in paracetamol poisoning, and it works best started inside the first 8 hours of an acute overdose. The blood paracetamol level is taken 4 hours after the ingestion. Waiting to see whether the child becomes unwell wastes the window.")
